\begin{proof}
Consider the set of subgroups of $G$ that contain $H$ but are not equal to $G$. This set is nonempty because it contains $H$. Since $G$ is finite, it has finitely many subgroups, so this set is finite. Choose a subgroup $M$ in this set maximal under inclusion. Then $M\neq G$ and $H\le M$. If $M<L\le G$ for some subgroup $L$ of $G$, then $L$ contains $H$. By the maximality of $M$ among the proper subgroups containing $H$, we must have $L=G$. Thus the only subgroups of $G$ containing $M$ are $M$ and $G$, so $M$ is a maximal subgroup of $G$ containing $H$.
\end{proof}

