\begin{proof}
Among all proper subgroups of $G$ containing $H$, choose one, say $M$, of largest order. Such a subgroup exists because $H$ itself is proper and $G$ is finite. Clearly $H\le M<G$. Suppose that a subgroup $L$ of $G$ contains $M$. If $M<L<G$, then $|M|<|L|$, while $L$ is a proper subgroup containing $H$, contradicting the choice of $M$. Hence every subgroup containing $M$ is either $M$ or $G$. Thus $M$ is a maximal subgroup of $G$ containing $H$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal P$ be the collection of proper subgroups $K$ of $G$ satisfying $H\le K$. The collection is nonempty since $H\in\mathcal P$. Since $G$ is finite, it has finitely many subgroups; therefore $\mathcal P$ is finite. Select $M\in\mathcal P$ maximal under inclusion. Then $M\neq G$ and $H\le M$. For any subgroup $L$ of $G$ with $M\le L$, we have $H\le L$. If $L$ were proper and strictly larger than $M$, then $L\in\mathcal P$, contradicting the maximality of $M$. Consequently, $L=M$ or $L=G$. Hence $M$ is a maximal subgroup of $G$ containing $H$.
\end{proof}
